WEBVTT

00:18.230 --> 00:22.380
Once, there was a boy who thought the world was a fairly simple place.

00:22.420 --> 00:27.420
He was born in 2007, into a family that had already imagined quite a lot of things for him.

00:27.460 --> 00:29.130
He did not know that, of course.

00:29.170 --> 00:32.860
At that age, the world was mostly made up of schoolbooks,

00:32.900 --> 00:36.040
examination results, holidays, karate classes,

00:36.080 --> 00:38.850
and the familiar faces of the people who loved him.

00:38.890 --> 00:42.470
His father moved to another country when he was five.

00:42.510 --> 00:46.210
The distance did not mean that his father disappeared from his life.

00:46.250 --> 00:50.880
He remained involved, sometimes more involved than the boy knew how to understand.

00:50.920 --> 00:53.030
There were conversations, expectations,

00:53.070 --> 00:57.230
instructions, and decisions that travelled across the distance between them.

00:57.270 --> 01:00.670
But the boy was young, and when his father came home,

01:00.710 --> 01:03.180
he was simply happy to see him.

01:03.220 --> 01:05.450
His childhood had a rhythm.

01:05.540 --> 01:08.680
His mother and grandmother would sit beside him for hours,

01:08.720 --> 01:13.190
teaching him Hindi and practically every other subject in his school curriculum.

01:13.310 --> 01:16.010
There was always another chapter, another exercise,

01:16.050 --> 01:18.500
another examination to prepare for.

01:19.070 --> 01:22.620
His holidays were rarely entirely his own.

01:22.660 --> 01:25.110
Much of his childhood revolved around marks,

01:25.150 --> 01:26.670
and he was good at collecting them.

01:26.710 --> 01:28.750
He was a topper until the eighth grade.

01:28.790 --> 01:32.280
He was also a child who cried when he was unprepared.

01:32.660 --> 01:36.700
Once, during an oral examination for a Hindi qualification,

01:37.250 --> 01:40.710
he sat before the examiner without having studied sufficiently.

01:40.750 --> 01:43.320
He became overwhelmed and started crying.

01:43.520 --> 01:46.150
The examiner felt sorry for him and passed him.

01:46.190 --> 01:49.720
It was a small incident, but perhaps it belonged to an earlier,

01:49.760 --> 01:54.330
gentler version of his life, when a child could cry in front of an adult and

01:54.370 --> 01:57.250
sometimes the adult would simply let him be.

01:58.310 --> 01:59.910
There was karate, too.

01:59.950 --> 02:02.420
Every time he received a new belt or moved up a rank,

02:02.460 --> 02:04.290
there was something to celebrate.

02:04.510 --> 02:08.060
After class, he and his father would sometimes go to McDonald's.

02:08.100 --> 02:14.450
A new belt meant food, conversation, and the simple happiness of having achieved something.

02:14.820 --> 02:16.360
He did not need much.

02:16.400 --> 02:18.210
He was happy when his mother taught him.

02:18.250 --> 02:22.180
He was happy when his grandmother helped him prepare for examinations.

02:22.220 --> 02:23.970
He was happy when his father returned.

02:24.010 --> 02:26.930
He was happy when he earned another belt.

02:26.970 --> 02:30.490
He did not yet understand that happiness could become something people expected him

02:30.530 --> 02:31.910
to justify.

02:32.030 --> 02:35.320
He did not know that one day he would look back at those years and wonder when

02:35.360 --> 02:38.990
exactly life had stopped feeling so uncomplicated.

02:39.230 --> 02:41.120
For the first twelve or thirteen years,

02:41.160 --> 02:43.120
he was simply a boy growing up.

02:43.160 --> 02:45.590
Then the world changed.

02:50.860 --> 02:53.710
He was in the eighth grade when the pandemic arrived.

02:53.750 --> 02:57.930
There are certain periods of history that can be explained through dates,

02:57.970 --> 03:01.290
statistics, and the names of cities.

03:01.330 --> 03:03.740
But the boy would remember those years differently.

03:03.780 --> 03:05.300
He would remember the telephone.

03:05.340 --> 03:08.880
Every call seemed to bring news of another death.

03:08.920 --> 03:14.030
Every conversation carried the possibility that someone familiar had become another

03:14.070 --> 03:19.300
name in a message, another photograph, another announcement that someone had passed away.

03:20.080 --> 03:22.150
People called one another to ask for news.

03:22.190 --> 03:24.190
They called to share news.

03:24.230 --> 03:29.170
Sometimes they called because they had nothing left to do except hope someone would pick up.

03:29.720 --> 03:31.740
The boy was twelve or thirteen.

03:31.780 --> 03:33.510
He should have been worrying about school,

03:33.550 --> 03:37.790
examinations, friends, and the ordinary inconveniences of adolescence.

03:37.830 --> 03:42.110
Instead, he was learning that people could disappear.

03:42.700 --> 03:46.140
One evening, at around nine, he spoke to his grandfather.

03:46.270 --> 03:48.260
It was an ordinary conversation.

03:48.300 --> 03:51.170
There was nothing about it that suggested it would become important.

03:51.210 --> 03:53.500
Nobody knew that they were approaching a final goodbye.

03:53.540 --> 03:56.380
Nobody stopped to memorise the words.

03:56.420 --> 03:58.990
By the next morning, his grandfather was dead.

03:59.280 --> 04:01.070
And there was a photograph.

04:01.110 --> 04:05.560
He still remembered the photograph of his grandfather inside an icebox.

04:06.020 --> 04:09.680
Some images do not become memories in the usual sense.

04:09.720 --> 04:14.180
They become something more permanent, something the mind refuses to put away.

04:14.310 --> 04:17.090
Years can pass, circumstances can change,

04:17.130 --> 04:21.960
and the rest of life can become busy, but that image remains remarkably clear.

04:22.450 --> 04:25.250
During those years, something else happened.

04:25.290 --> 04:29.560
For almost two years, his memory seemed to disappear.

04:29.630 --> 04:33.600
He would later say that he remembered absolutely nothing from that period.

04:33.640 --> 04:38.010
Not merely that the years were difficult or that he had forgotten some events.

04:38.050 --> 04:42.170
It felt as though an entire section of his life had gone missing.

04:42.410 --> 04:45.530
By 2022, he began coming back into motion.

04:45.570 --> 04:48.700
He returned to academics, worked through his school examinations,

04:48.740 --> 04:52.440
and began taking an active role in his own education again.

04:52.480 --> 04:54.280
His Term 2 board results were good.

04:54.320 --> 04:56.360
He brought his mother into the process,

04:56.400 --> 05:01.040
gradually rebuilt his routine, and tried to recover the person he had been before

05:01.080 --> 05:03.350
everything became unfamiliar.

05:03.920 --> 05:07.260
But the pandemic had changed the way he understood the world.

05:07.540 --> 05:11.540
Before it, death had been something that happened somewhere else,

05:11.580 --> 05:13.580
to people he did not know.

05:13.730 --> 05:17.250
After it, death was a conversation at nine in the evening.

05:17.290 --> 05:20.460
He had learned something at thirteen that some people manage to avoid learning for

05:20.500 --> 05:24.550
most of their lives: an ordinary day does not promise another ordinary day.

05:24.590 --> 05:28.610
And he never quite forgot it.

05:32.060 --> 05:38.510
Around fourteen or fifteen, he began understanding that his family had expectations

05:38.550 --> 05:41.240
that extended far beyond academic performance.

05:41.280 --> 05:43.990
He grew up in a deeply orthodox household.

05:44.030 --> 05:46.380
There were inherited ideas about religion,

05:46.420 --> 05:51.950
tradition, social conduct, and what a respectable young man ought to become.

05:51.990 --> 05:54.110
He was told not to talk to girls.

05:54.150 --> 05:58.690
He was told to keep away from them, that such distractions would affect his future,

05:58.730 --> 06:03.500
and that he had responsibilities towards his ancestors that he should not forget.

06:03.560 --> 06:06.330
He was still young enough to be forming his own opinions,

06:06.370 --> 06:11.200
but old enough to realise that some opinions were not particularly welcome.

06:11.510 --> 06:14.980
The expectations were rarely presented as possibilities.

06:15.310 --> 06:17.500
They were presented as instructions.

06:17.580 --> 06:21.410
By then, he had already begun understanding how much of his life had been organised

06:21.450 --> 06:23.520
around doing things correctly.

06:23.720 --> 06:24.810
Study well.

06:24.850 --> 06:26.220
Score well.

06:26.260 --> 06:27.840
Behave properly.

06:27.880 --> 06:29.810
Do not disappoint anyone.

06:29.880 --> 06:32.340
In the eleventh grade, he made a private decision.

06:32.380 --> 06:34.000
He would study on his own.

06:34.040 --> 06:38.200
He would not make his mother suffer because of his academic responsibilities.

06:38.240 --> 06:43.090
She had already done so much for him, and he wanted to take charge of his education

06:43.130 --> 06:47.220
without requiring her to spend every evening sitting beside him.

06:47.260 --> 06:48.220
He did.

06:48.260 --> 06:52.780
He studied, prepared, and eventually completed his school examinations.

06:52.820 --> 06:57.600
But there was one thing that would stay with him long after the examinations ended.

06:57.820 --> 07:02.010
His father had once questioned whether he was particularly intelligent.

07:02.050 --> 07:06.090
After an unsuccessful performance in an important entrance examination,

07:06.130 --> 07:10.850
he had said something along the lines of not believing that the boy was especially

07:10.890 --> 07:16.260
smart and doubting whether he would score well in his board examinations either.

07:16.680 --> 07:17.950
The boy remembered those words.

07:17.990 --> 07:20.550
He was sixteen when the board results arrived.

07:20.590 --> 07:23.960
Ninety-six point four per cent.

07:24.000 --> 07:27.930
There was a particular kind of silence that followed that result.

07:28.030 --> 07:31.480
The earlier doubts suddenly had less room to stand.

07:31.520 --> 07:35.390
His father, who had been so certain about what the boy could not achieve,

07:35.470 --> 07:38.990
was now considerably more pleased with what he had achieved.

07:39.030 --> 07:41.220
But something about the experience remained.

07:41.260 --> 07:44.020
He had wanted to be believed before the result,

07:44.060 --> 07:46.550
not merely congratulated after it.

07:46.690 --> 07:49.740
He had wanted someone to look at him and say that he could do it,

07:49.780 --> 07:52.600
without requiring him to produce a number first.

07:52.640 --> 07:54.340
It was a small distinction, perhaps.

07:54.380 --> 07:57.180
But some distinctions follow people for years.

08:02.360 --> 08:07.970
At seventeen, he faced a decision that would change the way he understood independence.

08:08.010 --> 08:12.090
He had received admission offers from several engineering institutions.

08:12.300 --> 08:15.250
There were universities he genuinely wanted to attend,

08:15.290 --> 08:20.690
including institutions that represented the possibility of a different life.

08:21.160 --> 08:24.070
There was the possibility of leaving home.

08:24.120 --> 08:27.510
For nearly a year, he begged his mother to let him go.

08:27.550 --> 08:30.050
He did not want to run away from education.

08:30.090 --> 08:31.940
He wanted to experience it differently.

08:31.980 --> 08:34.080
He wanted to study somewhere he had chosen,

08:34.120 --> 08:37.180
meet different people, live independently,

08:37.220 --> 08:42.160
and perhaps discover who he was outside the small circle of expectations that had

08:42.200 --> 08:44.110
followed him throughout childhood.

08:44.240 --> 08:46.460
He had been admitted to several places.

08:46.500 --> 08:49.080
He had worked for those opportunities.

08:49.120 --> 08:50.540
He had options.

08:50.580 --> 08:52.470
But his parents were afraid.

08:52.510 --> 08:55.230
They wanted him in a government institution.

08:55.270 --> 08:57.680
They believed it was the safer decision,

08:57.720 --> 09:02.890
the more reliable decision, perhaps the decision that carried the least uncertainty.

09:02.930 --> 09:05.440
His father was actively involved in the process.

09:05.480 --> 09:09.640
He was not a distant observer who had no influence over what happened.

09:09.680 --> 09:13.730
He participated in the decision, expressed his preferences,

09:13.770 --> 09:15.590
and made his position very clear.

09:15.630 --> 09:19.370
But the way those preferences were communicated mattered.

09:19.580 --> 09:23.390
The boy was made to feel that if he did not choose the institution his father

09:23.430 --> 09:26.980
wanted, he would be placing his entire future at risk.

09:27.020 --> 09:29.880
There were words to the effect of:

09:29.980 --> 09:32.680
If you don't go here, I don't know what will happen with your life,

09:32.720 --> 09:34.830
and I'm not responsible for it.

09:35.500 --> 09:39.210
It was a particularly difficult position for a seventeen-year-old.

09:39.250 --> 09:43.530
He was being told what to choose, while simultaneously being made to feel that the

09:43.570 --> 09:48.550
consequences of choosing differently would be entirely his responsibility.

09:48.790 --> 09:52.060
It was not a straightforward disagreement about universities.

09:52.490 --> 09:56.180
It was a decision wrapped in fear, guilt,

09:56.220 --> 09:58.640
and the weight of parental authority.

09:58.680 --> 10:00.950
He wanted his mother to understand him.

10:00.990 --> 10:02.750
He wanted her to let him leave.

10:02.790 --> 10:04.300
He kept asking.

10:04.340 --> 10:06.830
Eventually, the decision was made for him.

10:07.145 --> 10:09.775
And the irony was difficult to ignore.

10:09.955 --> 10:13.065
He had worked hard enough to get into several institutions.

10:13.105 --> 10:16.085
He had earned the opportunity to choose between them.

10:16.125 --> 10:18.985
But the very people who had encouraged him to study,

10:19.025 --> 10:24.285
compete, and perform had decided that the final choice would not belong to him.

10:25.215 --> 10:27.745
The first two semesters were miserable.

10:28.005 --> 10:29.805
He hated the college.

10:29.855 --> 10:32.655
He hated the circumstances that had brought him there.

10:32.855 --> 10:35.935
He hated the feeling of waking up every day knowing that he was living through a

10:35.975 --> 10:40.055
decision he had spent nearly a year trying to avoid.

10:40.495 --> 10:44.335
He would take long periods of leave, sometimes almost a month in a semester,

10:44.375 --> 10:47.285
and then return to college and attend regularly.

10:47.565 --> 10:50.995
Eventually, it became part of his identity there.

10:51.035 --> 10:52.655
People knew the pattern.

10:52.695 --> 10:54.975
He would disappear for a while, then return,

10:55.015 --> 10:59.595
attend classes, and carry on as though nothing particularly unusual had happened.

10:59.635 --> 11:01.235
But there was a reason.

11:01.275 --> 11:03.295
He did not want to be there.

11:03.345 --> 11:07.845
He began associating the institution with something far larger than lectures,

11:07.885 --> 11:11.015
examinations, or placement statistics.

11:11.375 --> 11:15.385
It became a reminder of a time when he had asked for something repeatedly and had

11:15.425 --> 11:16.715
not been heard.

11:16.755 --> 11:19.375
He would later describe it as a kind of trauma.

11:19.415 --> 11:24.375
And perhaps the most frustrating part was that he could not simply leave.

11:24.415 --> 11:26.075
He needed to finish the degree.

11:26.115 --> 11:27.325
He needed a job.

11:27.365 --> 11:29.285
He needed financial independence.

11:29.325 --> 11:33.775
He needed to build an exit that would not require him to ask anyone for permission.

11:33.815 --> 11:35.095
So he stayed.

11:35.135 --> 11:37.345
Not because he had made peace with the decision.

11:37.385 --> 11:40.245
Because he had not yet built the means to make another one.

11:45.125 --> 11:50.065
While all of this was happening, his mother was going through a difficult period of her own.

11:50.665 --> 11:55.625
For approximately two years, from 2022 to 2024,

11:55.665 --> 11:58.545
she had worked extraordinarily long hours.

11:58.585 --> 12:03.615
There were stretches when work seemed to consume almost the entirety of her day.

12:03.765 --> 12:06.755
She was trying to provide, trying to build security,

12:06.795 --> 12:10.555
trying to make sure that the future would be a little less uncertain.

12:10.595 --> 12:12.325
Then she stopped working.

12:12.365 --> 12:14.255
Her health deteriorated.

12:14.295 --> 12:17.745
At seventeen, the boy watched the woman who had once sat beside him for hours

12:17.785 --> 12:22.985
teaching him school subjects struggle with the ordinary movements of daily life.

12:23.195 --> 12:26.145
For a long period, she could barely move around the house.

12:26.185 --> 12:29.275
She could still walk, but slowly.

12:29.315 --> 12:31.785
Leaving her room became difficult.

12:31.825 --> 12:34.905
The pace of the household changed with her health.

12:34.945 --> 12:37.050
She was living with a serious muscular condition,

12:37.090 --> 12:39.960
and the consequences were felt throughout the family.

12:40.440 --> 12:44.670
His father spoke about the family's retirement plans and the implications of her

12:44.710 --> 12:46.040
leaving work.

12:46.080 --> 12:50.780
Sometimes he spoke as though her suffering had somehow become a financial

12:50.820 --> 12:52.230
inconvenience.

12:52.270 --> 12:57.030
On one occasion, he suggested that perhaps her suffering was connected to the family

12:57.070 --> 12:58.910
not praying to God.

12:58.950 --> 13:01.040
The boy could not tolerate that.

13:01.080 --> 13:02.900
He had spent years being told to behave,

13:02.940 --> 13:05.620
respect his elders, follow instructions,

13:05.660 --> 13:08.190
and understand that his parents knew better.

13:08.230 --> 13:12.080
But when his mother was struggling, something in him gave way.

13:12.120 --> 13:15.760
For the first time in his life, he shouted at his father.

13:15.800 --> 13:16.960
He told him off.

13:17.000 --> 13:21.050
He refused to remain silent while his mother was spoken about that way.

13:21.090 --> 13:22.510
His mother cried.

13:22.550 --> 13:25.930
He remembered it because it was the first time he had broken through the fear that

13:25.970 --> 13:28.350
had kept him quiet for so long.

13:29.080 --> 13:31.170
But there was no victory in it.

13:31.210 --> 13:32.580
He was still seventeen.

13:32.620 --> 13:34.260
He was still studying.

13:34.300 --> 13:36.560
His college problems had not disappeared.

13:36.600 --> 13:39.190
His mother's health had not suddenly improved.

13:39.230 --> 13:42.880
His family was still his family the following morning.

13:42.920 --> 13:46.600
He had simply discovered that there were things he could no longer watch without

13:46.640 --> 13:47.980
saying something.

13:48.140 --> 13:50.830
His mother had once been his safest person.

13:50.870 --> 13:54.700
She had taught him, supported him, helped him through examinations,

13:54.740 --> 13:59.450
and spent countless hours making sure that he was prepared for the world.

13:59.720 --> 14:05.650
Now he was watching her struggle with a world that had become particularly unkind to her.

14:06.020 --> 14:08.320
He did not know what to do with that reversal.

14:12.760 --> 14:17.040
There came a point when he could no longer maintain the appearance that everything was fine.

14:17.080 --> 14:20.350
He had continued studying, attending classes when he could,

14:20.390 --> 14:25.100
managing responsibilities, and trying to become the person everyone needed him to be.

14:25.140 --> 14:29.620
But the accumulated weight of everything had become difficult to carry.

14:30.000 --> 14:36.180
At eighteen or nineteen, he experienced a period of withdrawal that lasted forty-six days.

14:36.220 --> 14:38.880
His birthday happened during that episode.

14:38.920 --> 14:41.380
For forty-six days, he remained at home.

14:41.420 --> 14:42.790
He barely bathed.

14:42.830 --> 14:44.150
He did very little.

14:44.190 --> 14:47.640
He ate junk food, stayed in his room, and watched the days pass without much

14:47.680 --> 14:50.150
distinction between one and the next.

14:50.470 --> 14:54.060
The boy who had once organised his holidays around examination preparation could

14:54.100 --> 14:56.830
barely organise an ordinary day.

14:56.950 --> 14:58.530
Eventually, he broke down.

14:58.570 --> 15:03.160
He cried, and his mother realised that things had become serious.

15:03.230 --> 15:06.680
He received a diagnosis of depression and anxiety.

15:06.910 --> 15:08.040
Treatment began.

15:08.080 --> 15:13.350
He would never describe that period as a particularly graceful transformation.

15:13.400 --> 15:17.760
There was no sudden moment when he understood everything or woke up one morning

15:17.800 --> 15:19.630
feeling entirely different.

15:19.670 --> 15:23.850
There was simply a recognition that something had happened and that he needed help.

15:24.190 --> 15:26.020
His mother became involved again.

15:26.060 --> 15:29.510
She spoke to his college department and his Head of Department.

15:29.570 --> 15:33.230
She helped him return to classes, helped explain the circumstances,

15:33.270 --> 15:36.800
and made sure he could continue his education.

15:36.980 --> 15:40.440
He would remember this with enormous gratitude.

15:40.640 --> 15:44.280
There was also something else she did that mattered immensely to him:

15:44.320 --> 15:46.510
she protected his privacy.

15:46.670 --> 15:50.140
She did not turn his diagnosis into a public announcement.

15:50.200 --> 15:51.950
She told the people who needed to know,

15:51.990 --> 15:54.650
and he spoke to a couple of friends himself.

15:54.700 --> 15:59.740
For a boy who had spent years feeling that everything about him was being evaluated,

15:59.780 --> 16:05.060
having something so personal remain private was an extraordinary kindness.

16:05.190 --> 16:06.890
His mother had given him that.

16:06.930 --> 16:08.530
He would not forget it.

16:08.850 --> 16:11.580
He returned to college and began attending regularly.

16:11.620 --> 16:13.360
He got his attendance in order.

16:13.400 --> 16:15.520
He completed what was required.

16:15.560 --> 16:18.510
From a distance, it might have looked like he had recovered.

16:18.550 --> 16:21.170
But recovery was not a straight line.

16:21.210 --> 16:25.020
He could attend college and still dread entering the building.

16:25.060 --> 16:31.030
He could complete an assignment and still feel disconnected from the reason he had started.

16:31.140 --> 16:34.110
He could laugh with his mother and still have no idea how to tell her that he was

16:34.150 --> 16:34.600
struggling.

16:34.640 --> 16:36.890
He had learned to function again.

16:36.950 --> 16:39.740
He was still trying to learn how to live.

16:44.450 --> 16:49.420
His relationship with his mother slowly became something he did not quite recognise.

16:49.460 --> 16:52.620
There was a time when he could tell her nearly everything.

16:52.660 --> 16:54.510
He could complain about school.

16:54.550 --> 16:56.870
He could talk about what had upset him.

16:56.910 --> 17:00.770
He could disagree with her and expect that the conversation would eventually find

17:00.810 --> 17:02.570
its way back to normal.

17:02.820 --> 17:06.610
But as her health and emotional circumstances became more difficult,

17:06.650 --> 17:10.770
she sometimes felt that hearing his complaints was hurting her.

17:10.820 --> 17:13.890
And the boy decided that he would stop.

17:14.130 --> 17:16.700
He would not add to her burden.

17:16.740 --> 17:19.330
He would not tell her every little thing that went wrong.

17:19.370 --> 17:22.190
He would not bring every disappointment home.

17:22.230 --> 17:24.770
He would smile, make jokes, play around,

17:24.810 --> 17:29.450
ask about her day, and try to keep the atmosphere lively.

17:29.490 --> 17:32.170
He thought that at least one of them should be happy.

17:32.590 --> 17:36.350
That became one of the quietest decisions of his life.

17:36.390 --> 17:39.530
He stopped talking about many of the things that mattered to him,

17:39.570 --> 17:43.950
not because they had stopped hurting, but because he did not want his mother to hurt

17:43.990 --> 17:45.570
alongside him.

17:46.050 --> 17:47.810
He still loved her.

17:47.850 --> 17:49.470
He was still grateful for her.

17:49.510 --> 17:54.010
He still remembered the woman who had taught him every subject and helped him

17:54.050 --> 17:55.520
through childhood.

17:55.910 --> 17:57.500
But the relationship changed.

17:57.540 --> 17:59.930
They began behaving almost like roommates.

17:59.970 --> 18:02.680
They shared a home, ordinary conversations,

18:02.720 --> 18:06.010
small jokes, and the details of everyday life.

18:06.110 --> 18:09.860
But he no longer knew how to bring her the parts of himself that were difficult.

18:10.420 --> 18:13.410
He did not know how to tell her that he was frightened of losing her.

18:13.450 --> 18:15.140
Because he was.

18:15.180 --> 18:19.330
During the pandemic, he had learned how suddenly people could disappear.

18:19.420 --> 18:23.180
Now his mother was living with a condition that made the possibility of losing her

18:23.220 --> 18:25.440
particularly frightening.

18:25.800 --> 18:28.520
Whenever she mentioned her own death, even casually,

18:28.560 --> 18:31.320
his eyes filled with tears.

18:31.360 --> 18:33.530
He could not always explain why.

18:33.570 --> 18:37.560
It was as though his mind had already seen this particular ending and was desperate

18:37.600 --> 18:39.660
not to experience it again.

18:40.170 --> 18:43.640
He had spoken to someone at nine in the evening once.

18:43.680 --> 18:45.900
The next morning, that person was gone.

18:45.940 --> 18:49.470
He did not want another ordinary conversation to become a final one.

18:49.510 --> 18:51.440
And so he became careful.

18:51.640 --> 18:53.510
Perhaps too careful.

18:53.800 --> 18:56.230
There were things he wanted to say but did not.

18:56.270 --> 18:58.300
There were disagreements he avoided.

18:58.370 --> 19:03.400
There were conversations he never started because he could not bear the possibility

19:03.440 --> 19:05.300
that they might affect her health.

19:05.580 --> 19:09.220
He loved his mother, but he missed being able to talk to her.

19:09.260 --> 19:12.300
He missed the safe place she had once been.

19:12.340 --> 19:14.640
And he did not know how to get it back.

19:20.690 --> 19:22.260
His grandmother had helped raise him.

19:22.300 --> 19:26.270
She had taught him, sat beside him, and watched him grow into the child everyone

19:26.310 --> 19:29.840
knew as disciplined and academically successful.

19:29.880 --> 19:34.780
For a long time, he thought that relationship would remain one of the constants of his life.

19:35.020 --> 19:39.820
In 2023, there was a sentence she said to him before he left after a visit.

19:39.990 --> 19:43.080
She told him that even if he wanted to fight with his mother,

19:43.120 --> 19:44.590
he should not.

19:44.630 --> 19:47.620
She did not know how much longer she would be around.

19:47.660 --> 19:49.080
He remembered those words.

19:49.120 --> 19:53.070
It is strange how certain sentences remain with us,

19:53.110 --> 19:56.960
particularly when they are spoken by people we are afraid of losing.

19:57.180 --> 20:00.160
But their relationship became complicated.

20:00.380 --> 20:03.190
During his difficult period, she criticised him.

20:03.230 --> 20:06.510
She continued to compare him with the boy she remembered,

20:06.550 --> 20:10.230
the one who was disciplined, the one who scored perfect marks,

20:10.270 --> 20:13.050
the one who was expected to know how to behave.

20:13.160 --> 20:15.760
She had helped raise him into that boy,

20:15.800 --> 20:19.650
and perhaps she struggled to recognise the person who had emerged after everything

20:19.690 --> 20:20.880
that happened.

20:20.920 --> 20:22.470
He felt hurt.

20:22.510 --> 20:25.210
He also felt guilty for being hurt.

20:25.250 --> 20:28.930
There was gratitude for everything she had done and resentment for the way she

20:28.970 --> 20:30.480
treated him now.

20:30.630 --> 20:34.670
There was affection for the grandmother who had taught him and frustration towards

20:34.710 --> 20:38.380
the person who seemed unable to accept that he had changed.

20:40.010 --> 20:42.130
They sometimes went weeks without speaking,

20:42.170 --> 20:44.570
despite living in the same house.

20:44.760 --> 20:47.650
He did not always want to restart the conversation.

20:47.690 --> 20:50.550
He was afraid that another conflict might worsen her health.

20:50.590 --> 20:54.850
He had already seen how family conflict could affect people he loved,

20:54.890 --> 20:59.320
and he did not want to become the reason for another decline.

20:59.540 --> 21:00.760
So he remained quiet.

21:00.800 --> 21:04.690
He was beginning to discover that silence could preserve a relationship and damage

21:04.730 --> 21:06.580
it at the same time.

21:10.750 --> 21:14.200
There was a time when the boy cried whenever he was sad.

21:14.240 --> 21:16.650
Now he described himself as a rock.

21:16.690 --> 21:20.030
He could talk about things that had hurt him for years and then return to normal

21:20.070 --> 21:21.390
within minutes.

21:21.430 --> 21:24.280
Sometimes he would cry while explaining something and,

21:24.320 --> 21:28.520
almost immediately afterwards, feel as though the emotion had disappeared.

21:28.710 --> 21:31.360
He had become very good at containing things.

21:31.400 --> 21:33.530
He could smile in front of his mother.

21:33.570 --> 21:35.510
He could joke with people at college.

21:35.550 --> 21:39.180
He could appear perfectly ordinary when someone asked how he was doing.

21:39.310 --> 21:42.540
But he often felt as though he had become a closed book,

21:42.580 --> 21:44.930
a closed rock.

21:45.640 --> 21:48.430
He once compared himself to a blade of Damascus steel,

21:48.470 --> 21:53.790
folded, hardened, and etched in acid into something he had never intended to become.

21:53.830 --> 21:55.530
It was an image that made sense to him.

21:55.570 --> 21:59.170
He had been shaped by circumstances, expectations,

21:59.210 --> 22:03.760
disappointments, and the necessity of enduring things he did not know how to change.

22:03.800 --> 22:07.070
He had learned to anticipate the worst outcome.

22:07.320 --> 22:10.460
His thoughts ran at the speed of a supercomputer,

22:10.500 --> 22:16.290
calculating possibilities, failures, and every way an ordinary situation might go wrong.

22:16.660 --> 22:19.260
He had become extremely alert to disappointment.

22:19.300 --> 22:22.880
Even when something good happened, he struggled to remain with the feeling.

22:23.530 --> 22:25.160
He could achieve something substantial,

22:25.200 --> 22:29.160
acknowledge it for a moment, and move directly to the next task.

22:29.230 --> 22:31.300
An opportunity arrived.

22:31.590 --> 22:33.110
Okay, that's done.

22:33.150 --> 22:34.470
What's next?

22:34.510 --> 22:35.890
A difficult semester ended.

22:35.930 --> 22:36.690
Okay, that's done.

22:36.730 --> 22:37.410
What's next?

22:37.450 --> 22:39.570
A research project progressed.

22:39.610 --> 22:41.150
Another application was submitted.

22:41.190 --> 22:43.450
Another responsibility was completed.

22:43.490 --> 22:44.530
He kept moving.

22:44.570 --> 22:47.850
But he wondered whether he had stopped enjoying things along the way.

22:48.320 --> 22:52.560
There were two school friends with whom he still felt particularly comfortable.

22:52.600 --> 22:56.200
Beyond that, he often worried that people became annoyed with him.

22:56.240 --> 23:01.100
He learned to smile, make jokes, and behave as though everything was fine.

23:01.170 --> 23:06.710
Even jokes about his appearance and weight began affecting him more than he wanted to admit.

23:06.780 --> 23:12.430
He had become accustomed to being the person who made things easier for everyone else.

23:12.700 --> 23:17.020
He was less certain how to be the person who needed something.

23:17.060 --> 23:20.130
There were moments when anger broke through the numbness, too.

23:20.170 --> 23:24.160
One particularly difficult day, after missing his prescribed medication,

23:24.200 --> 23:29.100
a serious trigger pushed him into an overwhelming episode.

23:29.560 --> 23:31.290
He began throwing things around.

23:31.330 --> 23:35.380
The trigger had been significant, but the intensity of his response frightened him.

23:35.610 --> 23:39.518
It made him realise that being quiet did not necessarily mean that everything inside

23:39.558 --> 23:41.430
him had become peaceful.

23:42.020 --> 23:43.900
He continued treatment.

23:44.460 --> 23:49.120
The familiar drowsiness he had once experienced after taking his medication

23:49.160 --> 23:51.410
eventually became less noticeable.

23:51.450 --> 23:54.880
He sometimes wondered whether the medicine was working as it should,

23:54.920 --> 23:59.320
or whether he had simply become better at pushing everything down and continuing

23:59.360 --> 24:00.710
with his life.

24:01.470 --> 24:02.990
He knew he needed help.

24:03.090 --> 24:06.930
He also knew that finding someone with whom he felt sufficiently safe to discuss

24:06.970 --> 24:08.510
everything would take time.

24:08.550 --> 24:13.220
He had become a person who could describe his suffering more easily than he could

24:13.260 --> 24:16.290
actually share it.

24:20.040 --> 24:23.410
Through all of this, he had not stopped wanting a future.

24:23.450 --> 24:27.110
In fact, perhaps he wanted one more desperately than ever.

24:27.150 --> 24:30.290
He studied, built projects, explored research,

24:30.330 --> 24:34.600
pursued internships, and tried to develop skills that existed outside the boundaries

24:34.640 --> 24:36.330
of his college curriculum.

24:36.370 --> 24:38.640
He was interested in technology, research,

24:38.680 --> 24:42.620
and the possibility of building things that were genuinely useful.

24:42.990 --> 24:45.960
He wanted to be judged by what he could do.

24:46.070 --> 24:48.490
Not merely by the marks on a transcript.

24:48.530 --> 24:53.080
Not by how regularly he attended an institution he had never wanted to enter.

24:53.120 --> 24:56.990
Not by how closely he resembled someone's idea of a perfect boy.

24:57.030 --> 24:59.180
He wanted to be competent, independent,

24:59.220 --> 25:01.610
and capable of supporting himself.

25:01.860 --> 25:04.280
Most importantly, he wanted to leave home.

25:04.320 --> 25:06.830
Not necessarily to escape every person in it.

25:06.870 --> 25:08.700
He still loved his mother.

25:08.920 --> 25:12.120
He was grateful for his grandmother's place in his childhood.

25:12.160 --> 25:14.630
His relationship with his father was complicated,

25:14.670 --> 25:19.330
but his father remained an important influence in his life.

25:19.370 --> 25:21.750
He simply wanted some distance.

25:21.810 --> 25:25.690
He wanted a place where he could make decisions without having to account for every

25:25.730 --> 25:28.560
possible disappointment they might cause.

25:29.030 --> 25:32.590
He wanted to wake up in the morning and decide how the day would go.

25:32.630 --> 25:36.800
He wanted to speak to people without calculating whether his words might make

25:36.840 --> 25:38.410
somebody unwell.

25:38.450 --> 25:42.100
He wanted to disagree without feeling responsible for the consequences of every

25:42.140 --> 25:43.560
disagreement.

25:43.900 --> 25:47.540
He wanted to have a life that belonged to him.

25:48.130 --> 25:49.940
But leaving required money.

25:49.980 --> 25:51.320
Money required a job.

25:51.360 --> 25:53.250
A job required completing his degree.

25:53.290 --> 25:57.250
And completing his degree required continuing in a place that still reminded him of

25:57.290 --> 26:00.180
the decisions he had not been allowed to make.

26:00.300 --> 26:01.780
So he continued.

26:02.130 --> 26:03.140
He attended classes.

26:03.180 --> 26:04.500
He maintained his attendance.

26:04.540 --> 26:05.890
He worked on his projects.

26:05.930 --> 26:07.630
He searched for opportunities.

26:07.670 --> 26:11.870
He tried to build a future one small piece at a time.

26:12.010 --> 26:14.890
There was something almost cruel about the arrangement.

26:14.930 --> 26:20.570
He had to remain in a place that made him unhappy in order to acquire the means to

26:20.610 --> 26:23.170
leave the circumstances that made him unhappy.

26:23.210 --> 26:27.430
He was rowing towards freedom, but the oars were made of the very responsibilities

26:27.470 --> 26:29.350
he was trying to escape.

26:33.820 --> 26:37.730
Perhaps the most revealing sentence he ever said was not about college,

26:37.770 --> 26:41.060
his father, his grandmother, or the pandemic.

26:41.100 --> 26:43.440
It was a sentence about his mother.

26:43.600 --> 26:45.430
At least one of us should be happy.

26:45.470 --> 26:49.640
He had said it because he felt that sharing his problems was hurting her.

26:49.690 --> 26:53.030
But somewhere along the way, the sentence had become a philosophy.

26:53.070 --> 26:55.130
He would make sure his mother was happy,

26:55.170 --> 26:56.570
even if he was not.

26:56.610 --> 26:58.500
He would try not to upset his grandmother,

26:58.540 --> 27:00.140
even if he was hurt.

27:00.340 --> 27:02.730
He would satisfy the expectations placed on him,

27:02.770 --> 27:05.000
even if he did not agree with them.

27:05.040 --> 27:08.130
He would remain in college, even if he hated it.

27:08.320 --> 27:14.440
He would finish his degree, get a job, and leave only when he could do so on his own merit.

27:14.480 --> 27:18.700
He would smile in front of people, even when he had nothing to smile about.

27:18.880 --> 27:21.980
He would carry the unpleasant parts quietly.

27:22.150 --> 27:24.430
And whenever someone asked him how he was doing,

27:24.470 --> 27:29.280
he could give them a version of the answer that would not require a difficult conversation.

27:30.060 --> 27:32.280
But there was a problem with that arrangement.

27:32.580 --> 27:34.250
He was also a person.

27:34.290 --> 27:36.970
He had his own disappointments, ambitions,

27:37.010 --> 27:39.070
fears, and grievances.

27:39.110 --> 27:41.530
He had wanted things that did not happen.

27:41.570 --> 27:42.480
He had been frightened.

27:42.520 --> 27:44.310
He had been angry.

27:44.350 --> 27:46.160
He had been hurt by people he loved.

27:46.200 --> 27:50.570
He had spent years trying to ensure that the people around him were not burdened by

27:50.610 --> 27:51.780
his feelings.

27:51.820 --> 27:54.310
But where were those feelings supposed to go?

27:54.350 --> 27:58.580
Perhaps they went into the long absences from college.

27:58.620 --> 28:01.530
Perhaps they went into the forty-six days.

28:01.570 --> 28:03.300
Perhaps they went into the sudden anger,

28:03.340 --> 28:07.900
the sleepless nights, the tears that arrived whenever his mother mentioned death.

28:07.940 --> 28:11.840
Perhaps they went into the silence between him and his grandmother.

28:11.880 --> 28:16.560
Or perhaps they simply accumulated, one ordinary day at a time,

28:16.600 --> 28:20.050
until even he could no longer tell how much he was carrying.

28:20.090 --> 28:24.480
He did not need to hate his family to admit that some things had hurt him.

28:25.110 --> 28:30.120
He did not need to stop loving his mother to admit that their relationship had changed.

28:30.160 --> 28:34.360
He did not need to deny everything his grandmother had done for him to acknowledge

28:34.400 --> 28:37.190
that her words sometimes wounded him.

28:37.360 --> 28:41.430
He did not need to turn his father into a stranger to understand that his decisions

28:41.470 --> 28:44.390
and expectations had affected him deeply.

28:44.720 --> 28:48.600
Love and hurt had become occupants of the same house.

28:49.050 --> 28:50.800
Neither had agreed to leave.

28:54.770 --> 28:56.370
At night, when the house became quieter,

28:56.410 --> 28:58.990
he sometimes imagined himself rowing.

28:59.030 --> 29:02.850
There was darkness around him and a road through the landscape of his own mind that

29:02.890 --> 29:04.590
he used as an oar.

29:04.630 --> 29:06.160
Sadness was behind him.

29:06.200 --> 29:08.140
Something treacherous lay ahead.

29:08.180 --> 29:11.560
He was trying to cross a bridge, trying to cross a road,

29:11.600 --> 29:14.720
trying to reach a place where he could finally stand on his own.

29:14.760 --> 29:17.460
He did not know exactly what he was looking for.

29:17.500 --> 29:20.100
Maybe it was a small apartment in another city.

29:20.140 --> 29:24.600
Maybe it was a job that paid enough for him to support himself.

29:24.720 --> 29:29.770
Maybe it was a room where he could spend an entire evening without explaining his decisions.

29:29.810 --> 29:34.560
Maybe it was simply a morning when he woke up and felt that the day belonged to him.

29:34.600 --> 29:40.360
He was not looking to become a famous person or an extraordinary success.

29:40.400 --> 29:44.590
He did not need another examination result to prove that he was capable.

29:44.630 --> 29:48.260
He had already spent enough of his life proving things.

29:48.300 --> 29:52.470
What he wanted now was something much less impressive to the outside world.

29:52.510 --> 29:55.540
He wanted to enjoy a meal without having earned it.

29:55.580 --> 29:59.190
He wanted to pursue something because it interested him.

29:59.250 --> 30:02.950
He wanted to laugh without checking whether he was making too much noise.

30:02.990 --> 30:08.960
He wanted to be sad without worrying about who might become sad because of him.

30:09.280 --> 30:13.200
He wanted to have someone around whom he did not need to perform.

30:13.240 --> 30:18.110
He wanted to be able to say that something hurt without immediately explaining why

30:18.150 --> 30:20.420
he had no right to complain.

30:21.020 --> 30:25.370
And perhaps, most of all, he wanted to meet the person he might have become if he

30:25.410 --> 30:29.510
had not spent so much of his life trying to become what everyone else needed.

30:30.390 --> 30:34.120
Sometimes he remembered the little boy from before the pandemic.

30:34.460 --> 30:37.140
The boy who came home with a new karate belt.

30:37.180 --> 30:40.820
The boy whose mother and grandmother sat beside him for hours.

30:40.860 --> 30:43.490
The boy who was excited when his father visited.

30:43.530 --> 30:47.670
The boy who cried during an examination because he had not prepared.

30:48.240 --> 30:52.300
The boy who thought the world would be kind if he simply did everything correctly.

30:52.540 --> 30:55.640
He did not know whether that boy still existed.

30:55.680 --> 30:58.240
Perhaps he was buried beneath years of memories,

30:58.280 --> 31:01.190
expectations, disappointment, and fear.

31:01.390 --> 31:05.200
Perhaps he had grown older and changed beyond recognition.

31:05.590 --> 31:08.270
But sometimes, in the middle of an ordinary day,

31:08.310 --> 31:11.850
the older boy could still recognise something familiar.

31:12.040 --> 31:14.370
A small excitement about a project.

31:14.410 --> 31:15.960
A new idea.

31:16.000 --> 31:18.260
A good conversation with one of his friends.

31:18.300 --> 31:20.210
The possibility of going somewhere new.

31:20.250 --> 31:25.330
A future that might not look anything like the one his family had imagined.

31:25.480 --> 31:28.190
Those moments did not last very long.

31:28.230 --> 31:29.070
But they were there.

31:29.110 --> 31:32.630
He had once thought that his life would be measured by the marks he received,

31:32.670 --> 31:36.910
the institutions he entered, and the expectations he fulfilled.

31:36.950 --> 31:40.380
He was slowly discovering that none of those things could answer the question he had

31:40.420 --> 31:42.270
been carrying for years.

31:42.350 --> 31:46.400
Who was he, when nobody needed anything from him?

31:46.530 --> 31:48.330
He did not have an answer.

31:48.370 --> 31:49.490
Not yet.

31:49.530 --> 31:51.140
The boat continued moving.

31:51.180 --> 31:53.400
The boy did not know how far the shore was,

31:53.440 --> 31:56.630
and there were nights when he could not see it at all.

31:56.670 --> 31:58.210
But he kept rowing.

31:58.250 --> 32:00.560
Not because he had become invincible.

32:00.600 --> 32:03.030
Not because he had finally understood everything.

32:03.070 --> 32:05.550
Not because the past had stopped hurting.

32:05.660 --> 32:08.820
He kept rowing because somewhere, beyond the darkness,

32:08.860 --> 32:13.365
there might be a life in which he no longer had to explain why he deserved to be there.

32:13.405 --> 32:15.470
A life that was not a repayment.

32:15.510 --> 32:17.920
A life that was not an obligation.

32:17.960 --> 32:20.115
A life that was his.

32:20.155 --> 32:23.510
And for the first time in a long while,

32:23.550 --> 32:26.960
he allowed himself to imagine that he might reach it without having to become

32:27.000 --> 32:28.640
somebody else first.

32:28.680 --> 32:30.210
He did not know what he was.

32:30.250 --> 32:32.140
He did not know where he was.

32:32.180 --> 32:34.540
He only knew that he was still rowing.

32:34.580 --> 32:35.280
Still searching.

32:35.320 --> 32:36.540
Still here.
